<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Routines – Lesson # 1
    </h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 10px; font-size: 0.9em;">
        Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).
    </p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Sit Down.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Show comprehension of basic commands used in the classroom.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of keywords when hearing familiar basic commands (e.g., acting out the commands 'sit' and 'pick it up' as they are heard). Can respond to daily classroom commands like 'sit down' and 'pick it up.' Can respond to simple oral requests related to classroom routines when spoken slowly (e.g., 'Pick up the paper, please.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; border-bottom: 2px solid #1a5276; padding-bottom: 5px; margin-top: 30px;">
        The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages
    </h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Good Morning Song" with simple actions like standing up and sitting down. The teacher then says, "Good morning, everyone! Let's sit down, please." and gestures for students to sit at their desks. This connects to 'sit down' and 'please'.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a paper and says, "This is a paper." The teacher then drops the paper and says, "Oh no! Pick up the paper, please." The teacher models the action of picking it up. The teacher points to a chair and says, "This is a chair. Sit down on the chair, please." and models sitting. The teacher emphasizes the /p/ sound in 'pick' and 'paper' and the /d/ sound in 'down' and 'desk' while presenting objects (e.g., "This is a **p**en. /p/ pen.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(While sitting) "Am I standing or sitting?" (Students respond by showing 'sitting' action or pointing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(While picking up a pen) "Am I putting down or picking up?" (Students point to 'picking up' flashcard or mimic action).</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture cards showing classroom items (chair, desk, bag, pen, paper, notebook, door, eraser) and actions (sit, pick up, put, take, draw). Teacher-led story with simple commands and visuals.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and following simple spoken instructions related to classroom routines, using polite expressions.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Listen and Point":</b> The teacher says a word (e.g., "chair," "paper," "bag"), and students point to the corresponding picture card or real object in the classroom.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Listen and Show":</b> The teacher says a command (e.g., "Sit down," "Pick it up"), and students perform the action using Total Physical Response (TPR).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Yes or No Action":</b> The teacher states a command and performs a *different* action. Students respond with "No" or by shaking their head. The teacher then performs the *correct* action, and students respond with "Yes" or nod their head.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Imperatives ('Sit down', 'Pick it up', 'Put it on', 'Take the pen'), vocabulary (chair, paper, desk, pen, bag, notebook, eraser), prepositions (on, up, down), interjections (Please). Phonemic awareness: /p/ in 'pick', /d/ in 'down'; recognizing letter sounds A, B, C, D, E related to spoken words.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>"Listen and Act!" (Controlled Practice/TPR):</b> The teacher calls out basic commands slowly and clearly. Students listen and perform the action immediately. Examples: "Sit down, please.", "Pick up your paper, please.", "Put your bag on the chair.", "Take your pen." The teacher can also say words like 'pen' and 'door' and ask students to make the initial sound /p/ or /d/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>"Listen and Match!" (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher holds up two contrasting picture cards (e.g., one showing "sit" and one showing "stand"). The teacher says one of the commands, and students listen and point to the correct picture card. Extend with object vocabulary: "Point to the big paper. Point to the small paper." or "Point to the long pen. Point to the short pen."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>"Listen and Follow the Steps!" (Guided Practice):</b> The teacher gives simple two-step commands using sequencing words with numbers. Students listen and execute the commands. Example 1: "Step 1: Sit down. Step 2: Pick up your pen, please." Example 2: "First, stand up. Next, clap your hands." (This introduces "First," "Next," and "Step 1, Step 2").</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> A "Classroom Routine Leader" game where the teacher acts as the leader, giving a sequence of commands, and students follow the routine by listening and performing the actions collaboratively.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My Classroom Routine"</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains: "Let's do our classroom routine together! Listen carefully to my instructions."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a series of commands related to daily classroom setup and activities, focusing on fluency of response and task completion.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sequence: "Good morning, everyone! First, sit down, please. Then, pick up your pen. Next, draw a big circle on your paper. After that, put your pen down. Finally, pick up your paper and put it on your desk."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher monitors students, offering encouragement and repeating commands slowly if needed. The focus is on students' physical response to the commands.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Routine Leader" game and a short individual check-in.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives individual or small group commands (e.g., "Sit down, please." "Pick up the eraser, please." "Put the notebook on the desk.") and observes student responses through action.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehends and acts out 'sit down' command.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehends and acts out 'pick up' command with target objects (paper, pen, eraser).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to simple oral requests related to classroom routines (e.g., 'Put your bag on your chair, please.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows simple two-step commands (e.g., 'First, sit. Then, pick up.').</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me with your hands: Thumbs up if you understand 'sit down' and 'pick up' very well! Thumbs to the side if you understand a little. Thumbs down if you need more help."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one fun thing we did with our hands today?" (Students can point to a picture or mime the action).</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new action did you learn to do today with your body?" (e.g., pick up, sit).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to listen and do the actions today?" (Students can indicate with a finger gesture: easy-stretch arms wide, hard-make a small circle with fingers).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students understand the commands? (e.g., TPR, visuals, repetition).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What commands or vocabulary were challenging for students to comprehend? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more direct modeling, clearer pronunciation of specific sounds, breaking down multi-step commands).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did students respond to the phonemic awareness focus on /p/ and /d/?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we listened and did many actions in our classroom! Next time, we will *look* at pictures and *read* some of these words and actions together, like 'sit' and 'pick up', to understand our routine better."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>